{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "07-definite-integrals.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Area and the fundamental theorem",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Find the area under a curve: first approximately, with rectangles, then exactly, from an antiderivative, and see why an antiderivative gives it.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "What is the area under $y = x^2$ from $0$ to $1$? Geometry has formulas for rectangles and triangles, not for a curved edge. It is less than $\\frac12$, the triangle under the diagonal $y = x$, since the curve stays below the diagonal. Guess a number before reading on.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Rectangles",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Rectangles we can add. Cut the region into strips, and replace each strip by a rectangle as tall as the curve at the strip's right edge:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "clock t from 0 to 1\nview -0.1, 1.15, -0.1, 1.1\nF = graph(x^2, x, 0, 1) thick color 2\nAv0 = segment(0/5, 0/5 + 1/25*i) color 1\nAv1 = segment(1/5, 1/5 + 4/25*i) color 1\nAv2 = segment(2/5, 2/5 + 9/25*i) color 1\nAv3 = segment(3/5, 3/5 + 16/25*i) color 1\nAv4 = segment(4/5, 4/5 + 25/25*i) color 1\nAv5 = segment(5/5, 5/5 + 25/25*i) color 1\nAt1 = segment(0/5 + 1/25*i, 1/5 + 1/25*i) color 1\nAt2 = segment(1/5 + 4/25*i, 2/5 + 4/25*i) color 1\nAt3 = segment(2/5 + 9/25*i, 3/5 + 9/25*i) color 1\nAt4 = segment(3/5 + 16/25*i, 4/5 + 16/25*i) color 1\nAt5 = segment(4/5 + 25/25*i, 5/5 + 25/25*i) color 1\nBv0 = segment(0/10, 0/10 + 1/100*i) color 4\nBv1 = segment(1/10, 1/10 + 4/100*i) color 4\nBv2 = segment(2/10, 2/10 + 9/100*i) color 4\nBv3 = segment(3/10, 3/10 + 16/100*i) color 4\nBv4 = segment(4/10, 4/10 + 25/100*i) color 4\nBv5 = segment(5/10, 5/10 + 36/100*i) color 4\nBv6 = segment(6/10, 6/10 + 49/100*i) color 4\nBv7 = segment(7/10, 7/10 + 64/100*i) color 4\nBv8 = segment(8/10, 8/10 + 81/100*i) color 4\nBv9 = segment(9/10, 9/10 + 100/100*i) color 4\nBv10 = segment(10/10, 10/10 + 100/100*i) color 4\nBt1 = segment(0/10 + 1/100*i, 1/10 + 1/100*i) color 4\nBt2 = segment(1/10 + 4/100*i, 2/10 + 4/100*i) color 4\nBt3 = segment(2/10 + 9/100*i, 3/10 + 9/100*i) color 4\nBt4 = segment(3/10 + 16/100*i, 4/10 + 16/100*i) color 4\nBt5 = segment(4/10 + 25/100*i, 5/10 + 25/100*i) color 4\nBt6 = segment(5/10 + 36/100*i, 6/10 + 36/100*i) color 4\nBt7 = segment(6/10 + 49/100*i, 7/10 + 49/100*i) color 4\nBt8 = segment(7/10 + 64/100*i, 8/10 + 64/100*i) color 4\nBt9 = segment(8/10 + 81/100*i, 9/10 + 81/100*i) color 4\nBt10 = segment(9/10 + 100/100*i, 10/10 + 100/100*i) color 4\n> show F | The curve $y = x^2$ from $0$ to $1$.\n> show Av0, Av1, Av2, Av3, Av4, Av5, At1, At2, At3, At4, At5 | Five strips of width $\\frac15$; the $k$-th rectangle is $(k/5)^2$ tall. Each one pokes above the curve, so together they are too big.\n> hide Av0, Av1, Av2, Av3, Av4, Av5, At1, At2, At3, At4, At5; show Bv0, Bv1, Bv2, Bv3, Bv4, Bv5, Bv6, Bv7, Bv8, Bv9, Bv10, Bt1, Bt2, Bt3, Bt4, Bt5, Bt6, Bt7, Bt8, Bt9, Bt10 | Ten strips: the parts above the curve are thinner, and the total is closer.",
      "type": "scene",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Five rectangles of width $\\frac15$ and heights $\\left(\\frac15\\right)^2, \\left(\\frac25\\right)^2, \\dots, \\left(\\frac55\\right)^2$. `sum(f, k, a, b)` adds $f$ for $k = a, a + 1, \\dots, b$:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sum(k^2, k, 1, 5)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sum((k/5)^2 / 5, k, 1, 5)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$\\frac{11}{25} = 0.44$: too big, as the picture said. More strips poke out less.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Right Riemann sum, definite integral\n> Cut $[0, 1]$ into $n$ strips of width $\\frac1n$ and stand a rectangle of height $f(\\frac kn)$ on the $k$-th: the area of the rectangles is $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k=1}^{n} f\\!\\left(\\tfrac kn\\right) \\tfrac1n$. For a continuous $f$ these sums approach a single number as $n$ grows: the **definite integral** $\\int_0^1 f(x)\\,dx$, the area under the curve. On $[a, b]$ it is the same, with strips of width $\\frac{b - a}{n}$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let n = 10",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null,
      "slider": {
        "min": 1,
        "max": 60,
        "step": 1
      },
      "mode": "raw"
    },
    {
      "src": "let R = sum((k/n)^2 / n, k, 1, n)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "N(R)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Drag `n`. The sum is an exact fraction for every $n$, and its decimal falls toward a number from above: the rectangles stand above the curve. Which number? Even at $n = 60$ it is still $0.34\\ldots$, so the sums alone are slow to say.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The area so far",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "For the exact value, change the question: not the area up to $1$, but the area up to any $x$. Call it $A(x)$, the area so far. How fast does it grow as $x$ moves?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "clock X from 0.6 to 1.5\nview -0.1, 1.6, -0.15, 2.4\nF = graph(x^2, x, 0, 1.5) color 2\nLF = label(0.6 + 1.6*i, \"f(x) = x^2\") color 2\nE = segment(X, X + i*X^2) color 2\nA = graph(x^3/3, x, 0, X) thick color 1\nP = point(X + i*X^3/3) color 1\nLA = label(P, \"A(x)\") color 1\nS1 = segment(X + 0.1, X + 0.1 + i*X^2) color 3\nS2 = segment(X + i*X^2, X + 0.1 + i*X^2) color 3\n> show F, LF, E | The curve $y = x^2$, and the region under it from $0$ to $x$.\n> show A, P, LA; play X to 1.5 in 5s | Move $x$ and plot the area so far, $A(x)$, as it grows.\n> show S1, S2; play X to 1 in 3s | Push $x$ on by $dx$: the area gains a thin strip, of height $f(x)$ and width $dx$. So $dA \\approx f(x)\\,dx$: the slope of $A$ is $f$.",
      "type": "scene",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Push $x$ on by $dx$ and $A$ grows by a strip of height $f(x)$ and width $dx$, area about $f(x)\\,dx$ (the sliver between the strip's top and the curve is smaller still, of the order of $dx^2$). So $dA \\approx f(x)\\,dx$: the slope of the area so far is the height of the curve, $A' = f$. The area so far is an antiderivative, and lesson 5 finds those.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "For $f = x^2$: $A(x) = \\frac{x^3}{3} + C$, and $A(0) = 0$, since there is no area yet, so $C = 0$ and the area up to $1$ is $\\frac13$. Any antiderivative $F$ gives the same difference $F(1) - F(0)$: the $C$ cancels.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] Fundamental theorem of calculus\n> If $f$ is continuous on $[a, b]$ and $F' = f$ there, then $\\displaystyle\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx = F(b) - F(a)$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "`integrate(f, x, a, b)` finds a checked antiderivative, then evaluates it at the bounds. Step through it:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "integrate(x^2, x, 0, 1)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null,
      "stepwise": 0
    },
    {
      "src": "$\\frac13 = 0.333\\ldots$: the number the Riemann sums were falling toward.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Signed area",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "One arch of the sine wave, from $0$ to $\\pi$, has area exactly $2$:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "integrate(sin(x), x, 0, pi)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!mistake]\n> Twice the interval is not twice the area. From $0$ to $2\\pi$ the second arch lies below the axis, where $f < 0$: the strips $f(x)\\,dx$ count as negative, and the two arches cancel. A definite integral is a *signed* area.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "integrate(sin(x), x, 0, 2pi)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "integrate(x, x, 0, 2)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^2 x\\,dx$. (Check it against the triangle it is the area of.)",
      "hints": [
        "An antiderivative is $\\frac{x^2}{2}$; evaluate at $2$ and at $0$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "integrate(x^2, x, 1, 3)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_1^3 x^2\\,dx$.",
      "hints": [
        "$F(x) = \\frac{x^3}{3}$; the answer is $F(3) - F(1)$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "integrate(x^3, x, 0, 1)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Invent it: what is the area under $y = x^3$ from $0$ to $1$? Find the area-so-far function first.",
      "hints": [
        "$A' = x^3$ and $A(0) = 0$.",
        "$A(x) = \\frac{x^4}{4}$; the area is $A(1)$."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sum(2k - 1, k, 1, 6)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Add the first six odd numbers, $1 + 3 + 5 + \\dots + 11$.",
      "hints": [
        "Try $1$, then $1 + 3$, then $1 + 3 + 5$: what do the totals have in common?",
        "Lesson 1's growing square: from side $k - 1$ to side $k$ it gains two strips and a corner, $2(k - 1) + 1 = 2k - 1$ unit squares."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> Area is the number rectangle sums approach. The area so far grows at the rate of the curve's height, so it is an antiderivative, and the area from $a$ to $b$ is $F(b) - F(a)$, signed. Slope and area undo each other: that is the fundamental theorem, and the two halves of this course are one subject.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
